WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:01.373
- [Jeanne] I wanted to kick off

00:00:01.373 --> 00:00:03.210
with a question that I had on my own.

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:06.662
And that is, you know,
it sounds like the model,

00:00:06.662 --> 00:00:09.640
even, you know, there
were these four scenarios

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:12.188
where you showed these certain curves.

00:00:12.188 --> 00:00:17.188
And even with the strict
social distancing,

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:24.880
you still see a resurgence of the disease

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:30.340
sort of down the road,
like closer to December.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:33.410
Those curves were still
coming up later on.

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:37.233
So, do you mind going back to that slide?

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:39.003
- [Chloé] Sure.

00:00:39.003 --> 00:00:40.643
- [Jeanne] Just so that
everybody can visualize it.

00:00:42.421 --> 00:00:43.254
- [Chloé] This one?

00:00:43.254 --> 00:00:46.070
- [Jeanne] Yeah. So, curves
three and four, right?

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:49.860
So those are with the strict
social distancing measures.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:54.860
You know, are those, I
guess my question is around,

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:58.610
you know, what is the value
of social distancing now

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:03.040
if you're only going to
see those peaks take place,

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:04.400
but just at a later date?

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:07.828
- [Sinead] Yeah, so that's
a really great point.

00:01:07.828 --> 00:01:10.066
I guess one of the key values is that

00:01:10.066 --> 00:01:11.660
if you can delay it enough,

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:14.700
you can give governments
more time to respond and

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:15.960
something we also didn't mentioned is you

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:18.770
can give researchers more time

00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:21.512
to start looking into
different therapeutics,

00:01:21.512 --> 00:01:24.740
maybe start looking for vaccines.

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:25.760
So that's one thing.

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:27.630
You get more time to prepare so that

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:29.550
when it does eventually come around,

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:33.200
you may have a better outcome
because you are more prepared.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.200
But another, probably an
important assumption about

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:38.770
this paper is that when
that research happens,

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:41.430
they don't implement any new
social distancing measures

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:42.777
or any new kind of controlled measures.

00:01:42.777 --> 00:01:44.950
And so they just let things play out.

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:47.000
But in reality, what would probably happen

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:49.490
is that once you start
to see it increase again,

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:51.510
you may have governments that start to put

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:53.540
more restrictions back
in place just to try

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:56.360
and keep things a little
bit under control.

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:57.193
Yeah.

00:01:57.193 --> 00:01:58.026
- Great. Thank you.

00:01:58.026 --> 00:01:59.763
So, I think that was a really important,

00:01:59.763 --> 00:02:04.053
like buying time, I think, is the key.

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:08.912
- [Pedro] Yeah, that makes complete sense.

00:02:08.912 --> 00:02:12.860
Okay, and with that I think
we'll start going into

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:15.740
some of the questions that
our audience submitted

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:17.590
both through Zoom and Facebook.

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:20.870
And we have a first question that

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:25.140
was submitted by Rosalie here on Zoom.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:26.779
And she says that this feels that

00:02:26.779 --> 00:02:29.880
they're changing two variables at once

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:31.950
by adding different types of S.D.

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:33.960
but changing times as well.

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:35.810
Why not do one at a time and

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:39.293
for the same amount of time
before varying the time?

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:41.303
Does that make sense?

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:47.920
- Yeah, I think, so, here for example

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:49.403
for curve three and four,

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:53.380
it's actually the same
social distancing rules

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:55.800
but they implemented time by saying

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:58.697
for this third case we're going to stop

00:02:58.697 --> 00:03:01.280
at the end of March and the other one

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:02.490
is going to be at the end of April.

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:03.510
So they actually, I don't know if that

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:05.350
answered the question, but it's like

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:07.611
for the same social distancing rules,

00:03:07.611 --> 00:03:10.730
but with a different time of the end

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:12.213
of the social distancing rules.

00:03:12.213 --> 00:03:14.790
- So, they don't change
them at the same time,

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:17.140
they changed them in each group?

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:19.340
So, you know, the difference between

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:21.500
three, two, one are the increasing

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:22.833
amount of social distances and then

00:03:22.833 --> 00:03:25.300
the difference between
three and four is the time.

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:27.540
They're not being
changed at the same time.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:29.935
- Yeah, it's just, it's this line

00:03:29.935 --> 00:03:32.660
that Sinead presented where it's the same

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:35.440
social distancing rules,
but one is just ending

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:36.963
one month after the other.

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:40.960
So, if you compare those two scenarios,

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:43.380
you're still comparing the same
social distancing measures,

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:45.640
but what happens if we keep it,

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:47.933
... we could do.

00:03:49.284 --> 00:03:54.212
- [Pedro] And between two
and three, for example,

00:03:54.212 --> 00:03:59.212
the time also did change
a bit or is that not true?

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:00.800
- Yeah, so in between two and three,

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:03.810
they assume that we keep the
social distancing measures

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:06.430
one additional month and
we're even more strict

00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:09.200
by also adding this
general social distancing.

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:10.900
So it's true that in between two and three

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:13.480
you're like jumping a
lot because you're adding

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:16.033
one month of measures and
they're even stricter.

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:19.190
- Yeah. And I think that was the problem

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:20.610
that Rosalie was alluding to,

00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:22.280
was that in that particular jump,

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:24.460
you're actually changing a
lot of variables at once.

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:26.150
- [Chloé] Yeah. And
actually what you can see

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:29.010
from the result is by having more strict

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:31.570
socially distancing measure, you can,

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:35.100
here you see you decrease
the value of the peak.

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:36.863
So the number of infected people

00:04:36.863 --> 00:04:38.420
you're going to have at the peak.

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:40.940
But, if you just delay by one month,

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:42.240
by keeping the same measures,

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:45.240
you're going to have the
same value of the peak,

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:46.710
you're just delaying the peak.

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:48.657
- Yeah. So the time just delays it,

00:04:48.657 --> 00:04:50.915
but the amount of social distancing

00:04:50.915 --> 00:04:53.296
measures reduces the number of cases.

00:04:53.296 --> 00:04:54.279
- [Chloé] Yeah.

00:04:54.279 --> 00:04:55.577
- [Pedro] That makes sense.

00:04:55.577 --> 00:04:57.013
- And I also think it's worth noting

00:04:57.013 --> 00:04:58.720
that, you know, when we think about

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:01.046
experimental evaluation in
like a laboratory setting,

00:05:01.046 --> 00:05:03.490
it's a very controlled environment.

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:05.063
When we talk about public health,

00:05:05.063 --> 00:05:08.010
none of our lives is happening in

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:10.570
a controlled situation or in a vacuum.

00:05:10.570 --> 00:05:12.650
And I think that makes
the data a little messy

00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:14.517
just inherently, because of the fact that

00:05:14.517 --> 00:05:16.372
you can't control for,

00:05:16.372 --> 00:05:18.860
there's no control for human life, right?

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:20.950
And so, we all do what we're going to do.

00:05:20.950 --> 00:05:22.560
So I think, you know,

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:25.519
for these types of data,
it's tricky overall.

00:05:25.519 --> 00:05:29.047
Okay. So we have a question from Anissa.

00:05:29.913 --> 00:05:31.180
And the question is,

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:33.480
what happens if people come out too early

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:35.863
and how does free moving
restrictions too early

00:05:35.863 --> 00:05:38.620
lead to more people dying
or going back to phase one?

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:40.550
And which I think might be

00:05:40.550 --> 00:05:43.043
quite sort of explained here in this.

00:05:44.559 --> 00:05:47.230
But maybe you can go
through it for Anissa.

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:51.750
- Yeah, so once you
release these restrictions,

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:53.960
is when, you know,
people start moving about

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:55.530
as normal again and they can come into

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:57.260
contact with each other just as much

00:05:57.260 --> 00:06:00.300
as they could before those
rules were put in place.

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:02.130
And so, you have the
contact rate among people

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:04.750
starts increasing again
and like we showed,

00:06:04.750 --> 00:06:06.790
that means that the
transmission rate will increase.

00:06:06.790 --> 00:06:11.090
And that's why you can get
another increase in cases.

00:06:11.090 --> 00:06:12.930
So for example, the, you know,

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:15.360
the orange or the yellow lines here.

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:17.900
The rules were taken out
and it did take a while

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:19.220
for the cases to increase again,

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:21.480
but you do see that a few months later,

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:23.100
you see that second wave

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:25.750
because there's no more
controlled measures in place.

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:29.640
- Okay, great.

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:34.404
And third question from
Ann Sophie who is asking,

00:06:34.404 --> 00:06:39.010
why the curve
with the strictest model

00:06:39.010 --> 00:06:41.603
looks initially higher than the others.

00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:44.719
- I'm not sure that it does.

00:06:44.719 --> 00:06:48.430
- [Pedro] No, I think initially
higher before it goes down,

00:06:48.430 --> 00:06:51.023
I guess, there's a little
bleep there in January.

00:06:51.023 --> 00:06:51.856
- [Jeanne] Oh, I see.

00:06:51.856 --> 00:06:53.780
- [Pedro] That's probably
before the measures start

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:55.570
getting put in place, but I don't know if

00:06:55.570 --> 00:06:57.901
Chloe, Sinead, do you
want to address that?

00:06:57.901 --> 00:06:58.780
- Yeah, so that's well-spotted.

00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:00.340
But I think what's actually happening is

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:01.630
it's more of a plotting issue,

00:07:01.630 --> 00:07:03.530
so I think the orange
line and the yellow line

00:07:03.530 --> 00:07:05.130
are probably on top of each other.

00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:07.460
And what you're (mumbles) the yellow line.

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:09.730
Because, yeah, it's before
the measures were in place,

00:07:09.730 --> 00:07:13.650
so everything's happening the
same in those two scenarios.

00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:16.150
So it's just that you can't
see the line below it.

00:07:17.550 --> 00:07:20.400
- Great, thanks. So Juan was wondering

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:24.710
if we can elaborate on the impact of kids.

00:07:24.710 --> 00:07:26.480
So they had trouble
understanding the logic

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:29.569
behind why more cases would
be averted if children,

00:07:29.569 --> 00:07:31.820
I guess if there was social distancing

00:07:31.820 --> 00:07:34.320
incorporated for schools and nurseries.

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:37.450
But, I guess the assumption
that Juan is making

00:07:37.450 --> 00:07:39.550
is that children are less infectious.

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:42.863
Is that, maybe can you
elaborate on that a little bit?

00:07:43.890 --> 00:07:45.910
- Yeah, so the assumption
they make in the paper

00:07:45.910 --> 00:07:49.570
actually is that people
who are asymptomatic

00:07:49.570 --> 00:07:52.490
are less infectious
because they assume that

00:07:52.490 --> 00:07:55.300
if you have less symptoms
then you're less likely

00:07:55.300 --> 00:07:58.580
to transmit diseases,
which may not be true,

00:07:58.580 --> 00:08:00.510
but that's an assumption that they make.

00:08:00.510 --> 00:08:03.260
And so they assume that
because we haven't observed

00:08:03.260 --> 00:08:05.130
that many cases of
children, then children are

00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:08.290
more likely to be asymptomatic

00:08:08.290 --> 00:08:10.470
and so they're more likely
to be less infectious.

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:13.950
So that's what happens in this graph.

00:08:13.950 --> 00:08:15.570
And so you avoid more cases

00:08:15.570 --> 00:08:17.240
because you have less transmission,

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:19.450
for example, occurring in the household.

00:08:19.450 --> 00:08:22.620
So by keeping people home,
even if you close the school,

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:24.470
you still have interactions between adults

00:08:24.470 --> 00:08:26.710
and children in the households.

00:08:26.710 --> 00:08:29.300
And if children are less infectious,

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:31.550
then they transmit less diseases

00:08:31.550 --> 00:08:34.840
to the adults around them or
the other children around them.

00:08:34.840 --> 00:08:37.960
So I think that's why
they avoid more cases.

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:40.570
And you can also avoid more
cases in the older people

00:08:40.570 --> 00:08:42.760
because it's likely that
sometimes, you know,

00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:44.410
children live with their grandparents,

00:08:44.410 --> 00:08:47.503
or things like that, and
so you'll avoid cases.

00:08:50.540 --> 00:08:52.980
And one thing that
Sinead mentioned is that

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:54.570
at the end of the social distancing,

00:08:54.570 --> 00:08:55.780
people go back to normal.

00:08:55.780 --> 00:08:56.631
So kids still go to school

00:08:56.631 --> 00:08:59.280
and you can still have transmission.

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:00.930
So if children are less infectious,

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:02.853
then the transmission in the school is

00:09:02.853 --> 00:09:04.623
less likely to happen I guess.

00:09:07.464 --> 00:09:09.751
- Okay. I think we have
a question now about

00:09:09.751 --> 00:09:13.322
your actual work from
Lauren who loves calculus

00:09:13.322 --> 00:09:15.581
and who's wondering if and how

00:09:15.581 --> 00:09:17.933
you use calculus in your work.

00:09:21.093 --> 00:09:24.314
- I can say quickly. So
basically what we do is,

00:09:24.314 --> 00:09:29.020
or what I've done
previously, is instead of

00:09:29.020 --> 00:09:31.410
looking at people, we look at cells.

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:34.031
And so we use the same type of models

00:09:34.031 --> 00:09:37.145
that we're looking at cells in your body

00:09:37.145 --> 00:09:41.042
and, for example, when you
are infected by a virus,

00:09:41.042 --> 00:09:45.400
cells are acting in your
body to react to the virus.

00:09:45.400 --> 00:09:46.720
And a lot of things are happening

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:48.500
and we could look at
the dynamic of the cells

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:51.520
in the same way we could
look at dynamics of people.

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:52.840
And we're trained to understand

00:09:52.840 --> 00:09:54.963
those dynamics with mathematical models.

00:09:56.440 --> 00:09:59.084
- So I would agree completely with,

00:09:59.084 --> 00:10:00.000
I think Chloé described it really well.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:02.760
And just that, you know,
there's lots of options

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:04.050
depending on what you're interested in.

00:10:04.050 --> 00:10:06.410
You can use very similar models to look

00:10:06.410 --> 00:10:07.630
at the spread of diseases,

00:10:07.630 --> 00:10:10.210
you can look at cells becoming infected,

00:10:10.210 --> 00:10:12.989
but you can also look at
things like population growth.

00:10:12.989 --> 00:10:14.960
You can look at animal movements.

00:10:14.960 --> 00:10:17.253
There's so many different
things that you can do

00:10:17.253 --> 00:10:19.990
with math that I don't
think I was really aware of

00:10:19.990 --> 00:10:22.580
until I left school
and started studying it

00:10:22.580 --> 00:10:24.330
when I was older, so I
think it's really great

00:10:24.330 --> 00:10:26.048
to be interested now and

00:10:26.048 --> 00:10:28.470
there's lots of things you can do with it.

00:10:28.470 --> 00:10:29.974
- So adding onto Lauren's question.

00:10:29.974 --> 00:10:33.880
If a person is interested
in maybe learning how

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:35.110
to do this type of modeling,

00:10:35.110 --> 00:10:36.700
either because they just have an interest

00:10:36.700 --> 00:10:38.620
or maybe want to do this, you know,

00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:40.148
as an education and career,

00:10:40.148 --> 00:10:43.120
what types of maths do you think

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:45.485
they should try to hone in on?

00:10:45.485 --> 00:10:49.310
Like, for example, is calculus more

00:10:49.310 --> 00:10:52.253
or less valuable than say, statistics?

00:10:53.170 --> 00:10:54.360
You know, as people who come from

00:10:54.360 --> 00:10:56.506
those backgrounds in your training,

00:10:56.506 --> 00:11:00.070
what arm of math was sort of

00:11:00.070 --> 00:11:01.523
the most valuable for you?

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:06.660
- For me, I have a
background in mathematics,

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:09.149
like basic mathematics and then

00:11:09.149 --> 00:11:12.450
when I started trying
to apply into health,

00:11:12.450 --> 00:11:15.550
I realized I really needed
biostatistics and statistics.

00:11:15.550 --> 00:11:17.720
So, I would not neglect that.

00:11:17.720 --> 00:11:19.580
But, I'm a little bit biased because

00:11:19.580 --> 00:11:21.110
I have a PhD in biostatistics.

00:11:21.110 --> 00:11:22.480
So maybe I'm going to tell you that,

00:11:22.480 --> 00:11:25.100
but some other people would
tell you something else.

00:11:25.100 --> 00:11:27.210
But I think it's great to
have a first background

00:11:27.210 --> 00:11:29.404
in mathematics and be interested in

00:11:29.404 --> 00:11:32.100
biological health professions.

00:11:32.100 --> 00:11:34.430
There's always a way to
find a master's degree

00:11:34.430 --> 00:11:36.810
that does a little bit
more applied mathematics.

00:11:36.810 --> 00:11:38.310
And then you'll find a lot of people

00:11:38.310 --> 00:11:39.234
who are interested in your skills

00:11:39.234 --> 00:11:41.786
to try and understand
and help understanding

00:11:41.786 --> 00:11:44.810
biological processes
and mathematical skills

00:11:44.810 --> 00:11:46.730
or bioinformatics or, you know,

00:11:46.730 --> 00:11:48.840
even informatics is really useful.

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:50.823
So keep going that direction.

00:11:51.990 --> 00:11:53.460
- Yeah, my background is math too.

00:11:53.460 --> 00:11:56.440
And I really wish I had
taken more statistics.

00:11:56.440 --> 00:11:58.798
But also, computer
coding is something that

00:11:58.798 --> 00:12:02.010
I think both Chloé and I use all the time.

00:12:02.010 --> 00:12:03.170
I probably do that more now

00:12:03.170 --> 00:12:05.250
than I use my actual math knowledge.

00:12:05.250 --> 00:12:07.780
So, if there's any way
you can expose yourself

00:12:07.780 --> 00:12:12.320
to more computer coding, that's
also a great skill to have.

00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:13.500
- It's so interesting that

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:15.660
you're separating statistics from math.

00:12:15.660 --> 00:12:17.670
- [Pedro] I was thinking the same.

00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:19.248
- [Jeanne] I would consider them sort of

00:12:19.248 --> 00:12:21.633
statistics under the umbrella of math.

00:12:21.633 --> 00:12:22.466
- [Pedro] Exactly.

00:12:22.466 --> 00:12:24.123
- [Jeanne] So are you saying
that they're different?

00:12:25.131 --> 00:12:27.474
- [Chloé] Is it good to go into that?

00:12:27.474 --> 00:12:28.557
(laughs)

00:12:28.557 --> 00:12:30.900
- Okay, okay. We can
talk about that later.

00:12:30.900 --> 00:12:32.070
But, yeah that's really interesting.

00:12:32.070 --> 00:12:33.300
All right, let's get back to the paper

00:12:33.300 --> 00:12:36.370
so that we make sure everybody's
questions get answered.

00:12:36.370 --> 00:12:37.770
So here's a question from Peggy.

00:12:37.770 --> 00:12:39.960
And this is coming from Facebook.

00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:44.920
And it's around starting
school in September.

00:12:44.920 --> 00:12:46.390
Do you think that, you know,

00:12:46.390 --> 00:12:48.470
when people are applying these models,

00:12:48.470 --> 00:12:50.343
I guess maybe for Wuhan or

00:12:50.343 --> 00:12:54.150
for even places in the
United States like New York.

00:12:54.150 --> 00:12:57.440
Do you think, you know, opening up schools

00:12:57.440 --> 00:13:00.380
in September is going
to impact any of this?

00:13:00.380 --> 00:13:01.730
What are the models saying?

00:13:04.820 --> 00:13:07.240
- I can start answering, I don't know if Sinead you have a—

00:13:07.240 --> 00:13:09.600
I think we need more information.

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:12.767
Unfortunately, I don't
think we can predict now.

00:13:12.767 --> 00:13:14.830
That's actually why the Columbia team

00:13:14.830 --> 00:13:17.080
is only projecting in eight weeks

00:13:17.080 --> 00:13:19.390
because a lot of things are changing,

00:13:19.390 --> 00:13:21.942
whether it be the behavior of people

00:13:21.942 --> 00:13:25.200
or the policies, or like lot of things.

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:26.630
So we need more information.

00:13:26.630 --> 00:13:29.120
We also need more information
on how many people

00:13:29.120 --> 00:13:32.950
are actually infected in the
country or in New York City.

00:13:32.950 --> 00:13:35.370
So we need more testing
and that will also give us

00:13:35.370 --> 00:13:38.700
a better idea of, you know, how much

00:13:38.700 --> 00:13:41.113
the disease has spread in the city yet.

00:13:42.320 --> 00:13:44.430
And based on this
information, maybe we can

00:13:44.430 --> 00:13:47.060
reevaluate for the situation in September.

00:13:47.060 --> 00:13:49.080
But I definitely feel like it's too early

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:50.280
to answer that question.

00:13:53.614 --> 00:13:57.210
- Okay, all right. We'll
move on to the next one then.

00:13:57.210 --> 00:14:00.070
And this is a little bit outside
of the scope of this paper,

00:14:00.070 --> 00:14:03.203
but I still think we can
discuss this from Aileen,

00:14:03.203 --> 00:14:07.610
who, you know, in your model
the different conditions

00:14:07.610 --> 00:14:10.360
that people could have in
the model that was discussed.

00:14:10.360 --> 00:14:12.858
You mentioned that people were not

00:14:12.858 --> 00:14:15.634
infectious once they had recovered.

00:14:15.634 --> 00:14:18.580
And Aileen was wondering if
that had been determined,

00:14:18.580 --> 00:14:21.343
because she thought it was unknown.

00:14:22.950 --> 00:14:25.287
- So, it's very true because I would say

00:14:25.287 --> 00:14:28.710
it's unknown how long
people are infectious for.

00:14:28.710 --> 00:14:30.790
So, you know, the model they assume

00:14:30.790 --> 00:14:31.823
it's on average by the week,

00:14:31.823 --> 00:14:34.250
but it, you know, it could
go up to maybe two weeks,

00:14:34.250 --> 00:14:36.320
that's still definitely unknown.

00:14:36.320 --> 00:14:39.990
But the idea that, you know,
the way we define recovered

00:14:39.990 --> 00:14:42.960
is that you're no longer
infected and infectious.

00:14:42.960 --> 00:14:45.940
So the definition of recovered
would say that, you know,

00:14:45.940 --> 00:14:49.360
once you are in that recovered
phase, you are definitely

00:14:49.360 --> 00:14:50.920
not going to transmit the disease anymore.

00:14:50.920 --> 00:14:52.460
But, she's absolutely right.

00:14:52.460 --> 00:14:56.786
It's unknown at what point
you will move into that stage

00:14:56.786 --> 00:15:00.800
after you've infected
and become infectious.

00:15:00.800 --> 00:15:02.975
- And obviously then a
different question is,

00:15:02.975 --> 00:15:06.400
can people at that point
become infected again?

00:15:06.400 --> 00:15:07.463
But I don't think that

00:15:07.463 --> 00:15:09.210
that was really included in the models

00:15:09.210 --> 00:15:10.170
that we were discussing.

00:15:10.170 --> 00:15:13.720
That's a question for another paper.

00:15:13.720 --> 00:15:15.980
- Also, for that question I would refer to

00:15:15.980 --> 00:15:17.341
a previous D4P that was speaking

00:15:17.341 --> 00:15:19.486
about how long immunity lasts.

00:15:19.486 --> 00:15:21.500
And that's also something that can be

00:15:21.500 --> 00:15:23.660
included in the model when we know more.

00:15:23.660 --> 00:15:25.190
We can also assume that, you know,

00:15:25.190 --> 00:15:28.270
a proportion of the population
is likely to be reinfected

00:15:28.270 --> 00:15:29.697
if it's the case or not.

00:15:29.697 --> 00:15:31.630
And that can be adjusted in the model.

00:15:31.630 --> 00:15:33.803
But, if you want to
know more, go just watch

00:15:33.803 --> 00:15:35.123
the D4P from last week.

00:15:36.930 --> 00:15:39.670
- Well luckily that page is
live, so people can watch it

00:15:39.670 --> 00:15:41.600
and engage with the discussion guide.

00:15:41.600 --> 00:15:45.329
So we can share that with everybody here.

00:15:45.329 --> 00:15:48.250
Okay, so we have another
question from Lauren.

00:15:48.250 --> 00:15:51.070
And I guess this is getting
at the seasonal spread.

00:15:51.070 --> 00:15:53.445
So how does the virus spread differently

00:15:53.445 --> 00:15:55.760
when you're inside versus outside.

00:15:55.760 --> 00:15:58.160
For instance, can people
spending less time

00:15:58.160 --> 00:16:00.211
inside in the summer months

00:16:00.211 --> 00:16:03.223
prevent the virus from
spreading so quickly?

00:16:04.250 --> 00:16:05.363
If you know?

00:16:06.440 --> 00:16:09.020
- All right. So I am not an expert.

00:16:09.020 --> 00:16:11.410
I think a lot of it
depends on what happens

00:16:11.410 --> 00:16:13.260
to the virus in the air once, you know,

00:16:13.260 --> 00:16:14.680
somebody coughs or sneezes and

00:16:14.680 --> 00:16:17.140
how the particles kind of disperse.

00:16:17.140 --> 00:16:20.810
But, I think the general consensus

00:16:20.810 --> 00:16:23.477
or thought at the moment,
is that being outside is,

00:16:23.477 --> 00:16:26.410
you know, you may be less at risk

00:16:26.410 --> 00:16:29.770
because the particles have
more space to diffuse.

00:16:29.770 --> 00:16:32.448
But, yeah it's not my area of expertise

00:16:32.448 --> 00:16:34.615
so I wouldn't be able to say definitively.

00:16:39.190 --> 00:16:41.128
- Yeah, that's a very tough one to answer.

00:16:41.128 --> 00:16:44.250
But, you know, I guess
there's still research

00:16:44.250 --> 00:16:46.520
ongoing around all of this.

00:16:48.780 --> 00:16:49.920
Now, correct me if I'm wrong.

00:16:49.920 --> 00:16:53.978
But they did determine when
SARS or MERS first came out.

00:16:53.978 --> 00:16:57.073
Were those seasonal by any chance?

00:16:58.260 --> 00:17:00.030
- So, I think at least for SARS,

00:17:00.030 --> 00:17:03.983
it wasn't around long
enough for us to tell.

00:17:05.720 --> 00:17:08.750
But, so there are other
human coronaviruses

00:17:08.750 --> 00:17:10.933
that actually circulate year-round.

00:17:11.975 --> 00:17:15.380
So they're related to
the novel coronavirus.

00:17:15.380 --> 00:17:18.810
But, they're the second leading
cause of the common cold.

00:17:18.810 --> 00:17:21.280
So we've probably all had them a lot.

00:17:21.280 --> 00:17:23.140
And those are seasonal and, you know,

00:17:23.140 --> 00:17:27.230
there are higher numbers
of cases in the wintertime.

00:17:27.230 --> 00:17:30.010
But it's, you know, it
could be that the new

00:17:30.010 --> 00:17:31.970
SARS-CoV-2 is like that,

00:17:31.970 --> 00:17:35.350
but we just don't have enough
information to tell right now.

00:17:35.350 --> 00:17:39.193
- Yeah. So seems like we lost Chloé.

00:17:41.530 --> 00:17:44.452
But I think the only reason is

00:17:44.452 --> 00:17:46.960
I was going to suggest we go back to the

00:17:46.960 --> 00:17:50.170
slide where you had the
graphs for the next question.

00:17:50.170 --> 00:17:52.570
But I think we can
probably all remember it.

00:17:52.570 --> 00:17:55.500
And the question is from Aaron.

00:17:55.500 --> 00:17:58.590
And he's saying that
the maximum difference

00:17:58.590 --> 00:18:01.620
between the peak height when
you compare scenario one

00:18:01.620 --> 00:18:05.680
with scenario four is
about 20 percent or less.

00:18:05.680 --> 00:18:08.210
And that the total area under each curve,

00:18:08.210 --> 00:18:09.320
which is a concept that

00:18:09.320 --> 00:18:11.170
you might want to talk about as well,

00:18:11.170 --> 00:18:13.160
appears to be about the same.

00:18:13.160 --> 00:18:15.400
So he thinks that this suggests that,

00:18:15.400 --> 00:18:18.330
if assuming no effective
treatment is developed

00:18:18.330 --> 00:18:20.283
by December, the total number of deaths

00:18:20.283 --> 00:18:23.663
will actually be similar regardless of social distancing.

00:18:25.270 --> 00:18:27.530
- So they don't have, they don't share

00:18:27.530 --> 00:18:29.040
those exact numbers in the paper

00:18:29.040 --> 00:18:31.140
of all the total numbers of infections is.

00:18:32.490 --> 00:18:34.090
And so, yeah maybe I should go into,

00:18:34.090 --> 00:18:37.730
so the total area under
the curve in those graphs.

00:18:37.730 --> 00:18:39.560
So what each curve is plotting

00:18:39.560 --> 00:18:41.430
is the number of new cases each day.

00:18:41.430 --> 00:18:44.050
But if you were to add
up the number of cases

00:18:44.050 --> 00:18:46.578
on each day for the
whole length of the time,

00:18:46.578 --> 00:18:48.520
then that would be the
total number of cases

00:18:48.520 --> 00:18:51.020
that you get over the whole
period that they're looking at.

00:18:51.020 --> 00:18:54.130
And so that's essentially what
the area under the curve is

00:18:54.130 --> 00:18:57.050
is all those cases added up together.

00:18:57.050 --> 00:19:00.220
And so they may be very similar.

00:19:00.220 --> 00:19:02.860
I think in the paper they
plot the total number

00:19:02.860 --> 00:19:05.182
and they, you know, at December 2020

00:19:05.182 --> 00:19:08.190
right at the end when they
stop their simulations,

00:19:08.190 --> 00:19:10.130
there is a very slight difference between

00:19:10.130 --> 00:19:12.540
some of the curves, but it's not that big.

00:19:12.540 --> 00:19:14.590
And so, yeah, what that suggests is that

00:19:14.590 --> 00:19:16.810
the total number of cases
there isn't much difference

00:19:16.810 --> 00:19:19.501
between the scenarios, but you know,

00:19:19.501 --> 00:19:22.380
I think the main point is that, you know,

00:19:22.380 --> 00:19:23.390
even if that is the case,

00:19:23.390 --> 00:19:25.980
it's really important to delay that peak,

00:19:25.980 --> 00:19:28.540
because if you give people
more time to prepare,

00:19:28.540 --> 00:19:30.730
it may be that in a couple months' time,

00:19:30.730 --> 00:19:32.700
you find a therapeutic, or you know,

00:19:32.700 --> 00:19:34.610
your hospitals are more
prepared so you can

00:19:34.610 --> 00:19:38.060
actually have a better
impact when the peak starts.

00:19:38.060 --> 00:19:40.540
So you may actually be
able to push down that peak

00:19:40.540 --> 00:19:42.550
more than it is portrayed at the moment,

00:19:42.550 --> 00:19:45.500
assuming that, you know,
everything's the same.

00:19:45.500 --> 00:19:47.760
- Yeah, exactly. And
that the maximum number

00:19:47.760 --> 00:19:50.170
of cases in one day being slightly lower

00:19:50.170 --> 00:19:52.700
is obviously going to
make a big difference

00:19:52.700 --> 00:19:55.573
in the, you know, hospital
capacity for that day.

00:19:57.740 --> 00:20:00.014
- And as we sort of described before,

00:20:00.014 --> 00:20:04.550
those peaks are taking
place at a much later date,

00:20:04.550 --> 00:20:07.095
so the hope is that there's just more time

00:20:07.095 --> 00:20:08.334
- [Pedro] To prepare.

00:20:08.334 --> 00:20:10.700
- [Jeanne] For more
creative intervention. Yeah.

00:20:10.700 --> 00:20:12.760
I love this question from Sophia.

00:20:12.760 --> 00:20:14.790
And the question is around,

00:20:14.790 --> 00:20:16.980
can the researchers
use data from the study

00:20:16.980 --> 00:20:19.570
to map deaths due to COVID-19 over time?

00:20:19.570 --> 00:20:22.129
So what would be required to clarify

00:20:22.129 --> 00:20:25.283
the importance of social
distancing in these models?

00:20:34.846 --> 00:20:39.846
- Wait, is it about the
data that we need to gather?

00:20:39.960 --> 00:20:40.820
Or is it more about...

00:20:40.820 --> 00:20:44.870
- [Jeanne] Yeah, I
guess. I think, you know,

00:20:44.870 --> 00:20:47.360
we're in such early
stages of understanding

00:20:47.360 --> 00:20:49.210
the impacts of social distancing, right?

00:20:49.210 --> 00:20:54.210
So, what would help clarify,

00:20:54.370 --> 00:20:56.810
what extra data or, you know, variables

00:20:56.810 --> 00:21:01.112
are needed to really understand
how social distancing

00:21:01.112 --> 00:21:05.320
is impacting the spread of COVID-19?

00:21:05.320 --> 00:21:06.440
- Yeah, so I think we've been speaking

00:21:06.440 --> 00:21:09.350
a lot about testing in the population.

00:21:09.350 --> 00:21:11.420
Because now at the
moment, the observation,

00:21:11.420 --> 00:21:14.228
we have about people were
infected is mostly through

00:21:14.228 --> 00:21:17.003
people who are going to the hospital.

00:21:18.660 --> 00:21:21.930
So, it's definitely useful
to have a better idea

00:21:21.930 --> 00:21:23.210
of how many people are infected

00:21:23.210 --> 00:21:24.960
in the population to better understand

00:21:24.960 --> 00:21:29.810
how the virus has been
spreading in the population.

00:21:29.810 --> 00:21:32.610
I think what some people
have been saying is that

00:21:32.610 --> 00:21:34.460
we don't have a control because we don't

00:21:34.460 --> 00:21:36.971
know what's happening when
the disease is, you know,

00:21:36.971 --> 00:21:40.350
spreading if we don't do
the social distancing.

00:21:40.350 --> 00:21:42.420
But, we have seen that countries who

00:21:42.420 --> 00:21:43.960
haven't applied social distancing

00:21:43.960 --> 00:21:46.230
have been having some issues,

00:21:46.230 --> 00:21:47.753
which is definitely not a
control compared to like

00:21:47.753 --> 00:21:49.900
what happened in New York, for example.

00:21:49.900 --> 00:21:51.980
But it gives us insights on

00:21:51.980 --> 00:21:53.879
also the dynamic of the epidemic.

00:21:53.879 --> 00:21:57.450
And, also, like this
social distancing stuff

00:21:57.450 --> 00:21:59.050
is something that we would apply

00:21:59.050 --> 00:22:01.910
for any other disease
that's transmitted by air.

00:22:01.910 --> 00:22:03.790
It's not just specific to COVID-19,

00:22:03.790 --> 00:22:05.090
it's measures that have

00:22:05.090 --> 00:22:08.053
been recommended by
experts for a long time.

00:22:09.630 --> 00:22:11.693
I don't know if she knows
that's something to ask.

00:22:13.000 --> 00:22:14.220
- No, I think, yeah.

00:22:14.220 --> 00:22:15.855
- [Chloé] Yeah, cool.

00:22:15.855 --> 00:22:17.143
- [Pedro] All right.

00:22:17.143 --> 00:22:20.850
So this is a really
interesting question by Aditi.

00:22:20.850 --> 00:22:25.020
So okay, if individuals
are easily reinfected

00:22:25.020 --> 00:22:27.630
with the virus, and as we've
mentioned, that's a big if.

00:22:27.630 --> 00:22:29.732
And again, you know, directing people

00:22:29.732 --> 00:22:31.890
to last week's presentation.

00:22:31.890 --> 00:22:34.150
Thank you Lizzie for
sharing the link for that.

00:22:34.150 --> 00:22:36.330
But let's imagine hypothetically

00:22:36.330 --> 00:22:39.790
that if individuals are easily
reinfected with this virus,

00:22:39.790 --> 00:22:42.250
and they could then be infectious again,

00:22:42.250 --> 00:22:44.620
how would this affect the R0 value

00:22:44.620 --> 00:22:45.933
and the peak of the curve?

00:22:48.560 --> 00:22:52.010
Is that something that could
be included in the model?

00:22:52.010 --> 00:22:53.520
- Yeah so. Sorry, go ahead.

00:22:53.520 --> 00:22:54.717
- [Sinead] No, I'm fine.

00:22:54.717 --> 00:22:57.960
- [Chloé] So the R0 is
not going to be affected.

00:22:57.960 --> 00:23:01.709
The R0 is what happens
if the disease is spread

00:23:01.709 --> 00:23:04.910
in a population that's
completely susceptible.

00:23:04.910 --> 00:23:06.720
So, it's something that
you would determine

00:23:06.720 --> 00:23:09.567
most likely at the
beginning of the epidemic.

00:23:09.567 --> 00:23:13.310
The effective or productive
number, the Re or Rt or R,

00:23:13.310 --> 00:23:16.490
that Sinead mentioned, would
definitely be affected.

00:23:16.490 --> 00:23:18.010
Because people would just be like,

00:23:18.010 --> 00:23:23.010
instead of just being recovered
and not being reinfected,

00:23:23.400 --> 00:23:27.810
they will go back to their
susceptible compartment.

00:23:27.810 --> 00:23:29.710
And so that would definitely affect

00:23:29.710 --> 00:23:31.930
the progression of the epidemic.

00:23:31.930 --> 00:23:33.490
Yes, it would increase actually,

00:23:33.490 --> 00:23:35.640
the progression of the epidemic.

00:23:35.640 --> 00:23:39.663
And we would need a vaccine or
a treatment to prevent that.

00:23:42.830 --> 00:23:46.000
- So I'll ask the last question.

00:23:46.000 --> 00:23:48.010
And I'm just going to sort of summarize

00:23:48.010 --> 00:23:51.220
a couple of the ideas that
I see in our question chat.

00:23:51.220 --> 00:23:55.280
And it's around the motion to, you know,

00:23:55.280 --> 00:23:58.070
there's a lot of states that
are currently reopening.

00:23:58.070 --> 00:24:00.210
And what we're finding is, you know,

00:24:00.210 --> 00:24:02.409
some of the states that
are currently reopening

00:24:02.409 --> 00:24:04.368
didn't necessarily have very strict

00:24:04.368 --> 00:24:06.550
social distancing rules
in the first place.

00:24:06.550 --> 00:24:09.831
And that could be for a
variety of different reasons.

00:24:09.831 --> 00:24:14.831
And as they reopen, we're
also seeing things like,

00:24:15.160 --> 00:24:16.690
you know, everybody's
going to the beach or

00:24:16.690 --> 00:24:18.897
everybody's going to a
restaurant, you know.

00:24:19.830 --> 00:24:22.350
What do you think that that's going to do?

00:24:22.350 --> 00:24:24.540
Do you think that, you know,

00:24:24.540 --> 00:24:28.360
are there models that can help people

00:24:28.360 --> 00:24:30.200
sort of understand the consequences

00:24:30.200 --> 00:24:34.020
of those actions in these granular ways?

00:24:34.020 --> 00:24:38.739
And if so, you know, how
is that kind of information

00:24:38.739 --> 00:24:42.180
important for policy makers to know

00:24:42.180 --> 00:24:44.530
and sort of, Congresspeople to know?

00:24:44.530 --> 00:24:46.410
Where do you see those intersections

00:24:46.410 --> 00:24:49.890
of the modeling pieces and the people who

00:24:49.890 --> 00:24:54.350
are making the rules for society?

00:24:54.350 --> 00:24:55.900
I know that's a little
bit of a loaded question,

00:24:55.900 --> 00:24:58.250
but I would love to hear
your thoughts on that.

00:25:00.240 --> 00:25:01.790
- So, personally, I'm a little bit worried

00:25:01.790 --> 00:25:04.143
about places opening up too soon.

00:25:05.850 --> 00:25:09.620
Because, yeah, you know, cases may

00:25:09.620 --> 00:25:11.570
look like they're kind of steady now,

00:25:11.570 --> 00:25:13.168
but there's always a lag between

00:25:13.168 --> 00:25:15.640
when things are implemented
and then when we actually

00:25:15.640 --> 00:25:17.820
start to see cases turn up at the hospital

00:25:17.820 --> 00:25:19.650
because of the long period between when

00:25:19.650 --> 00:25:21.650
you start to show symptoms.

00:25:21.650 --> 00:25:23.570
And so what may look like, you know,

00:25:23.570 --> 00:25:25.160
things are going okay right now,

00:25:25.160 --> 00:25:26.840
it could be that in a few weeks' time,

00:25:26.840 --> 00:25:28.433
it's a different story.

00:25:31.340 --> 00:25:33.887
Yeah, sorry I forgot
the rest of the question.

00:25:33.887 --> 00:25:36.602
- Yes, like how are people engaging with,

00:25:36.602 --> 00:25:39.510
are people like in your
field, for example,

00:25:39.510 --> 00:25:41.260
or your lab group as an example,

00:25:41.260 --> 00:25:44.021
are they engaging with policy makers or

00:25:44.021 --> 00:25:47.380
people that are sort of
getting to say how people

00:25:47.380 --> 00:25:49.773
behave in a society or community?

00:25:52.707 --> 00:25:56.220
- I would say that I'm sure that people

00:25:56.220 --> 00:25:58.150
who are modeling these things are trying

00:25:58.150 --> 00:26:00.600
to engage with policy makers

00:26:00.600 --> 00:26:02.290
and people at the government level

00:26:02.290 --> 00:26:05.420
to try and help plan,

00:26:05.420 --> 00:26:07.580
or at least I'm sure
people are communicating

00:26:07.580 --> 00:26:08.413
the results of their model.

00:26:08.413 --> 00:26:10.390
But then it's up to policy makers,

00:26:10.390 --> 00:26:14.040
whether they want to implement, kind of,

00:26:14.040 --> 00:26:15.850
the recommendations of the scientists.

00:26:15.850 --> 00:26:17.780
Because obviously, there
are a lot of other factors

00:26:17.780 --> 00:26:19.110
that go into these decisions.

00:26:19.110 --> 00:26:21.157
And economic factors, and you know,

00:26:21.157 --> 00:26:22.700
lots of other factors.

00:26:22.700 --> 00:26:25.273
So, yeah.

00:26:26.220 --> 00:26:27.970
- [Jeanne] Yeah, I
imagine it's very tricky.

00:26:27.970 --> 00:26:29.070
Yeah, sorry, go ahead Chloé.

00:26:29.070 --> 00:26:30.110
- [Chloé] No, I was just going to say

00:26:30.110 --> 00:26:32.288
that we've seen decisions
that were based on models,

00:26:32.288 --> 00:26:35.423
like a couple of months ago in UK.

00:26:36.550 --> 00:26:39.260
Some decisions were
taken after the results

00:26:39.260 --> 00:26:42.784
from a model were
published or like spread.

00:26:42.784 --> 00:26:45.400
So, it's actually really happening,

00:26:45.400 --> 00:26:47.340
which is good that, you know,

00:26:47.340 --> 00:26:49.110
government are taking decisions

00:26:49.110 --> 00:26:50.410
that are based on science.

00:26:51.810 --> 00:26:52.980
And, again, as we said,

00:26:52.980 --> 00:26:56.188
the models are going to
be refined by more data

00:26:56.188 --> 00:26:59.410
and because we're learning
more about the disease.

00:26:59.410 --> 00:27:02.090
So hopefully it will
help also in the future

00:27:02.090 --> 00:27:04.030
to take, also, decisions that

00:27:04.030 --> 00:27:07.450
are balanced between the other factors

00:27:07.450 --> 00:27:09.460
and the number of people that have been

00:27:09.460 --> 00:27:11.163
affected by the disease.

00:27:12.350 --> 00:27:14.619
- Yeah, thank you. And I think with that,

00:27:14.619 --> 00:27:16.950
if anybody's really interested in science,

00:27:16.950 --> 00:27:19.990
but also interested in
public health and/or policy,

00:27:19.990 --> 00:27:22.150
I hope everybody out here will consider,

00:27:22.150 --> 00:27:24.710
you know, moving in those,

00:27:24.710 --> 00:27:26.021
like, training in science and then

00:27:26.021 --> 00:27:28.200
being a part of the government.

00:27:28.200 --> 00:27:30.560
I mean, how amazing could it be to see

00:27:30.560 --> 00:27:33.890
our government with people who value data

00:27:33.890 --> 00:27:36.857
and the scientific, you know, enterprise.

